WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.880
Politicians and bureaucrats spend a lot
of their time making new laws and regulations

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.860
to protect the public. But now that sh*t has really hit the fan. Even the government is realizing how

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:13.080
many of those are unnecessary. Here are 10 measures created in the name of public safety

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.800
that have been set aside in the name of public safety.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:20.360
The CDC testing monopolies perhaps the biggest and most obvious clusterf*ck on the list,

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:25.240
the agency mandated that only it could create and distribute tests and when they finally did roll them out,

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.860
they turned out to be painfully slow and
wildly inaccurate. Only after this colossal failure

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:35.460
did they allow private companies into the game, which has led to more testing kits that deliver faster results.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.340
If only that had happened months ago. HHS is issuing a regulation today

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.800
that will allow all doctors and medical professionals to practice across state lines.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.360
That is fantastic news except for the fact that HHS doesn't have that kind of authority.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.560
Each state has jurisdiction over who can practice there. Not surprisingly HHS has been pretty quiet

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:00.680
about the issue. But at least some states have waived restrictions making it easier for doctors to be doctors.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.300
You know things are serious when the TSA is easing stupid restrictions.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:09.620
Since 2006 the agency said that having more than 3.4 ounces of liquid in a container was super

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:14.020
dangerous on airplanes. But now that actual danger is around and no one is flying,

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:19.820
hand sanitizer in 12 ounce bottles is a-okay. Speaking of hand sanitizer here's some good news from the Alcohol

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:25.200
and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau, also known as the TTB because I don't know. Distilleries around the country

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.060
can now make hand sanitizer without jumping through a metric f*ckton of red tape

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.860
including permits, bonds, authorization, formula approval, and taxes.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:37.640
These waivers are only approved through June 30th however, after which that same hand sanitizer might

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:42.560
once again be very dangerous. Anyone who makes or sells alcohol is well-versed in ridiculous

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.140
and counterproductive regulation. But with the crushing blow social distancing

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.640
brings two restaurants at least one
strip of red tape is being snipped.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:53.340
State and local governments are lifting bans on alcohol home delivery which is

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.820
welcome news to bars, restaurants, and
anyone stuck at home paying attention to

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:00.960
the news. The FDA has been easing a bunch of restrictions including relaxing rules

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.660
on ventilator manufacturing, allowing
pharmacists to make hand sanitizer,

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.800
declaring previously unapproved
respirators as totally safe now,

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.240
allowing outside groups to make diagnostic tests, easing access to antiviral drugs,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.340
allowing the use of medical devices that remotely measure vital signs,

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:18.080
and allowing veterinarians to utilize telemedicine,

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.420
which was previously prohibited because I don't know. Medicare is now paying for telemedicine visits

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:26.280
which makes a lot of sense for people
who can't leave their house easily,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.020
or are at greater risk of infection, which come to think of it as basically everyone who is on Medicare

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:35.260
to begin with. HHS said that even though they don't fully conform to HIPAA rules regarding privacy

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.400
and security, doctors may now use Skype and FaceTime for telemedicine,

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.560
because the future is now! Or at least it was 17 years ago.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.020
Numerous states are freeing nonviolent offenders who were put behind bars

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:50.220
for technical violations or because
they simply couldn't afford bail.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.180
Cite and release policies are also being
enacted across the country,

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.340
keeping low-level offenders out of jail if there is no risk to the community and it's a

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:00.390
pretty damning admission by authorities
that for a long time they've been just

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:03.480
fine with locking people up who posed no
risk to the community.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.220
And plastic bags are back baby. After a hot and heavy fling with reusable grocery bags

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.640
politicians are waking up to realize
that canvas totes have a secondary

